course: dictionary
lesson: 253
title: Already: before now, or sooner than expected
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Already is a small word about time. It means before now, or before a certain time. «Italy already had pasta before Marco Polo» tells you that pasta was in Italy before the time Marco Polo arrived.
explain: Look at the situation around the word. In «Are you finished already?», the speaker is surprised that the job is done sooner than expected. The clue is that the word comes with a question about speed.
explain: In a sentence like «the grown-up teeth are already waiting», already means they are there before they are needed. Try swapping in "before now" or "before that time": if the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: Already means before now, or before a certain time.
fact: Already can also mean sooner than expected, as in «Are you finished already?»
fact: Already is an adverb: it tells you more about when something happens.
fact: «The grown-up teeth are already waiting» means they are in place before they are needed.
fact: A good check is to swap in "before now" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «Italy already had pasta before Marco Polo.» What does already mean here? | Only just | Never at all | Sooner than expected | *Before a certain time
exercise: Jake asks, «Are you finished already?» What is he surprised about? | That nobody finished at all | That it was done very quietly | That it took much longer than expected | *That it happened sooner than expected
exercise: Find and copy the one word from «If you have already faked a paper, telling a trusted grown-up now is better.» that shows something happened before now. | telling | paper | trusted | *already
test: «Jake gets a sword he already has.» What does already tell you? | He will get the sword next week | He lost the sword in the shop | *He owned the sword before this moment | He gave the sword to a friend
test: Which sentence uses already to mean sooner than expected? | The bus has no wheels at all | *The bus has come already, an hour early | The bus is blue and very old | The bus was quiet last week
exam: Which sentence puts already in the most natural place? | Homework already I have finished | I have finished already my homework | Finished I have already my homework | *I have already finished my homework
